# Erdős Problem #99 — activation-edge forest

## Statement

Start with a triangle-free global minimizer and a specified set \(R_0\) of strict
rattlers. Apply the monotone activation procedure from the canonical source-response
route: move one currently strict point at a time, keep every old active pair, and stop
at a boundary point where exactly one new active pair appears.

Call an activation a **unit activation** when its new pair is a unit contact. For each
member of \(R_0\) whose first active pair is a unit contact, record that contact, keeping
only one copy if the same contact is simultaneously the first contact of both endpoints.

> **Lemma (activation-edge forest).** The recorded unit contacts form a forest. Every
> recorded edge contains at least one member of \(R_0\), and if \(u\) members of \(R_0\)
> first activate through unit contacts, the forest has at least
> \[
> \left\lceil\frac u2\right\rceil
> \]
> edges.

## Proof

Order the recorded edges by the activation step at which they are created. At that
step the moved endpoint is a currently strict member of \(R_0\), so it has not been an
endpoint of any earlier recorded edge: a point that has acquired an active pair is no
longer strict and is never moved again by the procedure. Thus every new edge introduces
at least one vertex not present in the earlier recorded-edge graph. If both endpoints
are strict \(R_0\)-members, it introduces two new vertices; otherwise it introduces one.

Adding an edge with a new endpoint cannot create a cycle, so the final graph is a forest.
One recorded edge can be the first active pair for at most its two endpoints. Therefore
the number of recorded edges is at least \(\lceil u/2\rceil\). The endpoint statement is
part of the construction. \(\square\)

## Quantitative consequence

The canonical activation budget gives, for an initial strict-rattler set of size
\(\rho\), at most \(2\pi D\) members whose first active pair is a diameter pair. After the
same-hull extension loss of at most \(5\widehat p_H\), the activation-edge forest in the
extended representative therefore has at least

\[
\frac12\max\{0,\rho-5\widehat p_H-2\pi D\}
\]

edges. This is a structural refinement of the persistent unit-activation transfer: the
unit trace is not merely a large collection of contacts, but an explicitly ordered
forest embedded in the final triangle-free contact graph.

## Exact limitation

The forest can be long, can have rootless components formed by a first contact between
two strict rattlers, and can later be supplemented by additional contact edges. A unit
forest of this size is compatible with triangle-freeness, and the forest need not be a
bridge in the final contact graph. Consequently the lemma does not yet give either

\[
|Q|-|N_A(Q)|\ge \widehat p_H+1
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
\dim\ker S\ge\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}.
\]

The missing bridge is still a theorem charging this activation forest to the source
Hall deficiency or to independent null directions of the boundary Schur response.
